Output-stationary on a 32×32 array took 1389 cycles with no stalls. That matches my fold model: 5 folds × (216 + 64) ≈ 1400 cycles. On the same model, other output-stationary shapes come out slower. A 64×16 array needs 6 folds, about 1780 cycles, and a 16×64 array needs 9 folds.

Weight-stationary on 32×32 is the only candidate I estimated as close. It needs 7 reduction folds, each streaming 144 rows, for a rough total of 1450 to 1680 cycles depending on fill overhead. I'll simulate it to see whether it beats 1389.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Both runs land within about 2% of each other: output-stationary took 5759 cycles with 4764 stall cycles, and input-stationary took 5879 cycles with 4844 stall cycles. The stall counts are almost the same, which fits a fixed cost of moving the 36,864 weight values in from memory. That works out to about 7.7 values per cycle at either dataflow.

Changing array shape within output-stationary or input-stationary would mostly change the roughly 1000 compute cycles and leave the stalls about where they are. The only dataflow I haven't tested is weight-stationary. It loads each weight into the array once and keeps it there, so its memory traffic follows a different pattern. My estimate for weight-stationary on 64×64 is about 2290 compute cycles (12 folds of about 191 cycles). That is worse on paper, but if it overlaps the weight loads with compute better, it could cut the stalls by more than it adds. I'll run it to find out.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The simulator returned 12,433 cycles at 21.7% utilization. Of those, 8,730 were stall cycles, which leaves about 3,700 cycles of compute. That matches my estimate, so the os mapping itself is fine. The design is limited by memory bandwidth.

The filter tensor holds 864 × 128 ≈ 110k values. That likely won't fit in the 64 kB filter SRAM, so reducing data refetches is what will lower the cycle count. The ws dataflow loads each weight into the array only once, so its traffic and stall pattern could be quite different. I'll test ws on the same 32×32 array to see whether its lower stall count makes up for its longer compute time.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The ws dataflow took 56,065 cycles, 4.5 times slower than os. Its compute portion was about 12.8k cycles, close to my estimate, and stalls were higher at 43k. ws runs 108 passes, and each pass reloads weights and spills partial sums. That traffic is what makes it bandwidth-bound.

Input-stationary (is) still needs testing. On a 32×32 array it maps the 864-value dimension onto rows (27 passes) and the 25 output pixels onto columns (1 pass). Each input and each filter value is fetched only once, so total traffic could be lower than os, where the input is re-read for each of the 4 passes over the 128 channels. The cost is partial-sum writeback across the 27 passes. Its compute estimate is about 6k cycles, so is beats os only if it stalls much less. I'll test is on 32×32 to check.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 64×16 os array took 11,649 cycles, the best result so far. Stalls fell to 4,114, less than half of the 8,730 on 32×32. Compute rose to about 7.5k cycles, which matches 8 channel passes of roughly 940 cycles each. This shows the stalls depend mostly on how many weights the array needs per cycle across its width. Halving the width from 32 to 16 cut the stalls by more than it added in compute.

A 32×16 array keeps the same width of 16, so the filter bandwidth demand and the 8 channel passes stay the same. All 25 output pixels still fit in one row pass. With 32 rows instead of 64, each pass needs less pipeline fill and drain, and fewer rows sit idle. Compute should come to about 8 × 912 ≈ 7.3k cycles, with stalls close to those of 64×16. I'll use my last call on 32×16 os. If it doesn't beat 11,649 cycles, I'll submit 64×16.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The input-stationary run on 64×8 took 118,007 cycles, three times worse than output-stationary on 8×64 (39,265). Of those cycles, 113,328 were stalls. With M = 8, the array holds only a small piece of A at a time, so the design has many folds and refetches B for each one. That rules out input-stationary.

The output-stationary result suggests a bandwidth floor. B has 196,608 elements, and 196,608 ÷ 39,265 ≈ 5 elements per cycle. If that is the effective bandwidth, any design that reads B exactly once lands near 39k cycles. Weight-stationary is the remaining option. It loads each B tile into the array once and streams the 8 rows of A past it. On a square 32×32 array that is 24 K-folds × 8 N-folds = 192 folds of about 72 compute cycles each, roughly 14k cycles of compute. This test shows whether weight-stationary hides more of the B transfer behind compute than output-stationary does, or at least matches it.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary on 8×32 took 39,335 cycles, which is within 0.2% of 8×64 (39,265). Halving the tile cut stalls by about 3,000 cycles, but doubling the folds added about the same amount of compute. Total time stays near 39k, so my double-buffering idea doesn't explain the stalls. The likely limit is the fixed memory bandwidth: B has to be streamed in once, at about 5 elements per cycle. Output-stationary seems to sit at that floor no matter how wide the array is.

The one shape I haven't tested is a taller output-stationary array, 16×64, which uses the full 1024-PE budget. With M = 8, half its rows would sit idle. It would still show whether the simulator's bandwidth or fold handling depends on array height. If it doesn't beat 39,265, I'll submit 8×64 output-stationary.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary on 16×64 also took 39,265 cycles, exactly matching 8×64. Adding rows changes nothing because M = 8 leaves the extra rows idle, and the run is bound by streaming B in at the fixed memory bandwidth. The three output-stationary runs all landed between 39,265 and 39,335 cycles, which looks like that bandwidth floor. Weight-stationary and input-stationary were about three times slower. Another output-stationary shape is unlikely to beat 39,265, so I'll keep the last simulator call unused.

I'm submitting 8×64 output-stationary. It ties 16×64 for the lowest cycle count, uses half the PEs, and has twice the utilization (7.8% vs 3.9%).

{"answer": {"array_h": 8, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 8×64 output-stationary (OS) array took 497 cycles. That is consistent with 3 column folds (192/64) of about 166 cycles each. Within OS, the other shapes look worse: 8×32 doubles the folds, and a taller array only adds fill latency.

Input-stationary (IS) is the main alternative. It would hold K=96 down the rows and M=2 across the columns, then stream all 192 columns of N. On a 64×8 array that gives 2 folds of about 264 cycles each, roughly 528 cycles. That estimate is close enough to 497 that it needs a real simulator run.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The input-stationary (IS) run on 64×8 took 651 cycles, more than my estimate of about 528. The difference fits a fill cost of about 2·h per fold: 2 × (2·64 + 8 + 192 − 2) = 652. With that correction, IS can't beat output-stationary (OS). A 32×8 array would need 3 folds of about 262 cycles, roughly 786 in total.

Weight-stationary (WS) is the only dataflow I haven't run. My model puts its best shape, 64×64, at about 6 folds (2 over K, 3 over N) of 130–190 cycles each, so 780–1150 cycles. That is likely worse than 497, but my IS estimate was off by about 23%, so I'll use a spare call to check it.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary was much worse: 56,185 cycles, of which 50,166 were stalls. With only 25 output pixels, each weight fold does very little compute, so there's no work to hide the weight loads behind. That rules out ws.

For os on 32×64, each of the 2 filter folds needs 864 × 64 ≈ 55 kB of weights. That is close to the full 64 kB filter SRAM, so the next fold's weights probably can't be prefetched while the current one computes. A 32×32 array still covers all 25 output pixels in one fold along its height. It splits the 128 filters into 4 folds of about 27.6 kB each, which fit in half the buffer. That should let loads overlap with compute. Compute rises to about 3,700 cycles, but stalls should drop by more than that.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary on 32×32 came in at 12,433 cycles, only 356 fewer than on 32×64. Stalls fell by about 2,140 cycles, but compute rose by about 1,790. Both runs still spend most of their time waiting on the 110,592 bytes of weights. That points to roughly 10 bytes per cycle of memory bandwidth, so os has a floor of about 11,000 cycles.

Input-stationary (is) is the one dataflow I haven't tested. It maps the 864 reduction terms down the array height and the 25 output pixels across the width, then streams the 128 filters through over time. On a 64×32 array that means 14 folds. Each fold streams only about 8 kB of weights against 128 or more compute steps, so loading and compute should overlap much better than in ws. Ws did badly because each fold had only 25 compute steps to hide its loads behind.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Input-stationary on 64×32 took 21,129 cycles, of which 17,126 were stalls. That is worse than output-stationary (os), so os remains the best dataflow. The best result so far is os on 32×32 at 12,433 cycles.

When I halved the os array width from 64 to 32, stalls fell by about 2,140 cycles and compute rose by about 1,790, for a net gain of 356 cycles. Halving again to 32×16 still covers all 25 output pixels in one fold. It splits the 128 filters into 8 folds of about 13.8 kB of weights each, which should overlap loading with compute better. Compute roughly doubles to about 7,400 cycles. If the weight-load time stays near 11,000 cycles and overlaps almost fully with compute, the total could fall below 12,433. If not, I'll submit 32×32.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output stationary (os) on the 8×64 array took 86,553 cycles. Of those, 81,526 were stall cycles, so only about 5,027 cycles went to compute, close to my estimate of about 5,076. The design is limited by memory traffic, not compute. Under os, each column fold streams a 64 × 768 slice of the filter, about 48 kB, which likely won't fit alongside the prefetch buffer in the 64 kB filter SRAM.

Input stationary (is) keeps the 1×768 input fixed in the array. Each row fold then streams a 64 × 384 filter slice, about 24 kB, which fits in the 64 kB filter SRAM with room for prefetching. I'll test is on a 64×8 array next.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Both runs stalled for about 81,000 cycles. Output stationary (os) on 8×64 stalled 81,526 cycles and input stationary (is) on 64×8 stalled 80,814, even though their compute work differs. The cause is likely the filter itself. It holds 768 × 384 ≈ 295k elements, and with M = 1 nothing reuses it, so every dataflow has to stream the whole filter from DRAM once. At the fixed bandwidth that takes about 81,000 cycles. The remaining cycles, about 5,000 for os and 6,200 for is, look like compute that doesn't overlap with those memory transfers.

Weight stationary (ws) is the one untested dataflow. It does more compute: a 32×32 array needs 288 weight tiles. But it loads weights tile by tile, so it could overlap compute with the filter stream better and finish near the memory floor. I'll check that next.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output stationary (os) on 8×32 took 88,369 cycles, which is 1,816 more than 8×64. Halving the width cut stalls by 2,828 cycles (81,526 to 78,698), but compute rose by 4,644 cycles (about 5,027 to 9,671). So the wider array wins under os, and 64 is the widest allowed width.

The one os variant left to check is a taller array at that width. 16×64 uses the full 1,024-PE budget. With M = 1, the extra rows add no useful compute and should cost only about 8 cycles per fold. Each row may add its own read port, though, so the simulator might fetch data faster and stall less. If 16×64 doesn't beat 86,553 cycles, I'll submit 8×64 os.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 32×64 output stationary run took 86,505 cycles with 81,334 stall cycles. That is 64 cycles worse than 64×64 os at 86,441. All six simulator calls are used. The best measured design is 64×64 output stationary, so I'm submitting it.

Every design except weight stationary finished within about 1% of 86.4k cycles. That floor comes from streaming the ~295k weights through the fixed memory bandwidth. Weight stationary was about 4× slower, at 353,943 cycles.

{"answer": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The first run took 13,645 cycles, and stalls caused 11,698 of them. The remaining 1,947 compute cycles match my estimate, so the ws mapping is good, but memory bandwidth limits the design.

The total off-chip traffic should be about the same for every dataflow. Input is about 50k elements, filters 6k and outputs 75k, and the input and filter tensors each fit in their 64 kB SRAM. The dataflows differ in when they need that data. Output-stationary keeps partial sums inside the PEs and writes each output tile once at the end, which spreads the output traffic out. That could hide more of the stall time even though its compute count is higher. I'll test os on the same 64×64 array to compare.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary cut stalls from 11,698 to 9,210 cycles. Its compute time rose from about 1,950 to about 4,940 cycles, though, so the total came out slightly worse: 14,149 cycles against 13,645 for ws. Both designs end up close to the same total, so the limit is how fast memory can deliver the total data, and the dataflow mainly decides how much compute overlaps with that transfer.

Input-stationary (is) is the remaining dataflow to try. It loads input tiles once and streams the small filter tensor (about 6k elements) past them. That puts the heavy input traffic into the stationary loads instead of a continuous stream, which may overlap better with compute than ws did. I'll test it on the same 64×64 array so the three dataflows compare directly. After that I'll use the remaining calls to see whether a smaller array lowers stalls.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Input-stationary is much better: 5,693 cycles in total, with only 1,976 stall cycles. That is about 3,700 compute cycles and 20.7% utilization. Keeping the large input tensor stationary means each input tile loads once as a burst. Only the small filter tensor (about 6k elements) has to stream, so most memory traffic overlaps with compute.

On the 64×64 array, the 64 rows match K = 64 exactly, and the 784 pixels need 13 column passes. Each pass starts by loading a 4,096-element input tile, and those bursts probably cause most of the remaining stalls. A 64×32 array keeps the exact K match and halves each tile load. The cost is about 25 passes instead of 13, so compute time goes up. I'll test whether the lower stall time makes up for that.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Input-stationary on 32x8 came back at 118,007 cycles, three times worse than output-stationary on 8x32 (39,335). Stalls made up 110,184 of those cycles. Keeping the input resident did not reduce the traffic that causes stalls, so output-stationary on 8x32 remains the best result so far.

Weight-stationary is the only dataflow I haven't tested, and my fold estimate for it may be as unreliable as my estimate for output-stationary was. I'll test it on a square 16x16 array. That maps 16 values of K to the rows and 16 values of N to the columns, and the inputs stream through 8 rows of M per fold. If it is also stall-bound, I'll spend the remaining calls on output-stationary shapes.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary on 16x16 came back at 124,815 cycles, with 83,344 of them stall cycles. All three dataflows are stall-bound. Output-stationary on 8x32 is still the best result at 39,335 cycles.

I'll spend the remaining calls on output-stationary shapes. My fold model assumed the simulator maps M to array rows. If it maps the dimensions the other way, a 32x8 array would put N=256 on the rows in 8 folds and M=8 on the columns. That could change the order of memory traffic and the stalls. Testing 32x8 shows which orientation the simulator uses.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary on 32x8 came back at 25,791 cycles with zero stall cycles. That is the best result so far, and it shows the simulator maps the dimensions the opposite way from my fold model. Because this design has no stalls, it is limited by compute, and 23.8% utilization means the folds carry a lot of overhead.

Of the output-stationary shapes that fit the 256-PE budget and haven't been tested, only 16x16 uses all 256 PEs. The other untested ones (8x8, 8x16, 16x8) have fewer PEs and would likely be slower while compute-bound. 16x16 changes the fold count and fill overhead, but it might bring stalls back the way 8x32 did. I'll test it, and if it isn't better I'll submit 32x8.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary on 16x16 came back at 39,297 cycles, with 26,530 of them stall cycles. That puts it level with output-stationary on 8x32 and well behind output-stationary on 32x8 at 25,791 cycles with no stalls. Of the shapes I tried, only the tall, narrow array avoided stalls.

The results suggest that swapping the array's orientation decides whether a design stalls. Swapping output-stationary from 8x32 to 32x8 removed every stall. Input-stationary on 32x8 spent only about 7,800 of its 118,007 cycles on compute (118,007 minus 110,184 stall cycles). If flipping it to 8x32 removes its stalls the same way, it could get close to 7,800 cycles. I'll spend the last call on that. Even if it is worse, I can still submit output-stationary on 32x8.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 8, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Input-stationary on 8x32 came back at 28,991 cycles with zero stalls. Flipping the orientation did remove the stalls, as I expected. The cycle count is still far above my estimate of about 7,800, so that estimate was wrong.

All six simulator calls are used. Output-stationary on 32x8 is the fastest design I measured: 25,791 cycles with no stalls. Input-stationary on 8x32 is next at 28,991 cycles. I'm submitting output-stationary on 32x8.

{"answer": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The simulator reported 88,369 cycles. That splits into about 9,671 compute cycles, which matches my 9.7k estimate, and 78,698 stall cycles. So the workload is memory-bound. The 768×384 filter is about 295k elements and every dataflow has to read all of it, so stall behaviour matters more than array shape.

Weight-stationary (`ws`) may hide memory traffic better: each fold loads a weight tile once and needs very few compute cycles because M = 1. Its total compute is higher than `os`, at roughly 1,152 folds × 33 cycles ≈ 38k cycles. Its stall profile could be quite different, though, so I'm testing a square 16×16 array next.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Input-stationary on 32×8 took 88,419 cycles, 50 more than output-stationary on 8×32 (88,369). It split into about 10.9k compute cycles and 77,524 stall cycles. Two dataflows with different array shapes both finished within 0.06% of each other at about 88k. That points to a memory-bandwidth limit: streaming the 295k-element filter likely sets the minimum, whatever the array mapping.

One more output-stationary run will test that limit. On a 16×16 array, output-stationary roughly doubles the compute estimate to about 24 folds × 800 ≈ 19k cycles. If the total stays near 88k, the workload is memory-bound and 8×32 `os` is the final answer. If the total rises by the extra compute, 8×32 `os` also wins.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Stalls make up 42.5k of the 56.8k cycles. Compute alone took about 14.3k cycles, which matches my estimate, so this design is limited by memory traffic. The filter is the likely cause. It holds 864 × 128 ≈ 110k elements, which is larger than the 64 kB filter SRAM. With output stationary on 16 rows, the 25 output pixels split into 2 row tiles, so the whole filter gets streamed in twice.

A 32×8 array fits all 25 pixels in one row tile. It still runs 16 column passes (128 / 8), so compute stays about the same, but the filter should only stream in once. That should roughly halve the filter traffic.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The simulator returned 6,301 cycles. Only about 977 of those were compute, which matches my estimate, and the other 5,324 were stalls. That means memory bandwidth limits this design, not the array shape.

The filter matrix is the likely cause. It holds 256×192 = 49,152 elements, about 48× the input matrix, and every dataflow has to bring it in from memory at least once. OS on an 8x64 array consumes 64 filter values per cycle, so it outpaces memory and stalls. IS on a 64x8 array streams N=192 past the stationary inputs and needs only about 8 filter values per cycle. Its compute is similar, about 1,050 cycles over 4 folds. This test will show whether lower filter demand reduces stalls or whether total filter traffic sets a floor that every design hits.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

IS on 64x8 came in at 6,103 cycles, 198 fewer than OS. The stall count fell from 5,324 to 4,800 even though compute went up from about 977 to about 1,303 cycles. Both designs stall for roughly the time it takes to stream the 49,152 filter values from memory. That points to a floor set by total filter traffic, plus some compute and pipeline-fill time that doesn't overlap with it.

That makes weight-stationary (WS) worth testing despite its high compute estimate. WS loads each filter value into the array once, as a preload ahead of each fold. Those preloads may overlap with compute instead of stalling it. If that happens, total cycles would be close to whichever is larger, compute (about 4,800) or the filter-streaming time (also about 4,800). That would beat the current results, which pay for both. A 32x32 array uses the full budget and has as few folds as any WS shape (8 K-folds × 6 N-folds = 48), so it's the best WS shape to try.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The OS run on 8x32 came in about 3% faster than IS on 32x8 (5,319 vs 5,489 cycles). Stalls account for most of the time in both runs (3,712 and 4,128 cycles), so moving the 36,864-element filter dominates. Dataflow mainly decides how much of that load overlaps with compute.

For OS, 8x32 is already the widest shape the 256-PE budget allows, because the minimum height is 8. That makes it the best OS layout for spreading N=384 across columns. WS is the dataflow I haven't tested. It keeps filter tiles stationary in the array. On 32x8, K=96 splits into exactly 3 row folds and N splits into 48 column folds. Each fold reloads weights, so compute will be worse. But WS may stream the filter more evenly against the fixed bandwidth, so I'll test it before committing to OS.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

WS on 32x8 had no stalls, but it took 10,223 cycles. Its 144 weight folds (3 along K × 48 along N) cost about 71 cycles each. Every WS shape within the 256-PE budget still needs 144 folds: 16x16 gives 6 × 24, and 8x32 gives 12 × 12. Even with no stalls, those shapes should land around 7k cycles, which loses to OS at 5,319.

OS on 8x32 is still the best result. Its compute is about 1.6k cycles, and roughly 3.7k cycles go to filter-fetch stalls. I'll use one call to check whether that stall time is fixed by bandwidth or depends on the shape. OS on 16x16 doubles the N folds to 24 but changes how the filter is fetched per fold.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

OS on 16x16 is the best result so far at 4,201 cycles. Its compute rose to about 3.0k cycles because N now takes 24 folds. Stalls fell from 3,712 to 1,178 cycles, which means stall time depends on the array shape and is not fixed by bandwidth. A square array asks for fewer filter elements per cycle, so the fixed bandwidth keeps up better.

OS on 32x8 would likely do worse. Its 48 N folds would push compute to about 6k cycles, more than any stall savings. IS on 16x16 is the better test. K=96 fits in 6 folds, with N=384 streaming through each, for roughly 2.5k cycles of compute. Its per-cycle filter demand drops to 16 elements, half of what IS on 32x8 needed. If its stalls fall the way the OS stalls did, it could beat 4,201.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The simulator returned 53,783 cycles. Compute accounts for only about 19.9k of those, which matches my estimate, and stalls account for 33.9k. The run is memory-bound. With weight-stationary on 16×16, the 64 input channels split into 4 K-folds. Each fold writes partial sums that later folds have to read back and add to. The full output is 784 × 96, about 75 kB, which is larger than the 64 kB output SRAM, so those partial sums likely spill to DRAM.

Output-stationary keeps each output's accumulation inside its PE and writes each output only once. Its compute estimate is higher, at about 28k cycles. It could still finish faster if it removes most of the stalls, so I'll test it next.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 8×32 output-stationary design took 11,409 cycles with no stalls, about 39% more than my ~8,200 estimate. Work-based utilization (MACs ÷ (PEs × cycles)) is only about 57%, below the reported 77%. That suggests a fixed or per-pass overhead outside the compute phase that my estimate left out.

The next-best estimate is input-stationary on a 32×8 array, at about 10,400 cycles. It uses a different dataflow and loads fewer values per pass (160 output channels per window instead of 288 filter-window values). If the extra overhead scales with how many values stream through each pass, this design could beat 8×32 output-stationary, so I'll test it next.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 32×8 input-stationary design took 73,057 cycles. Of those, 58,568 were stalls, which means memory bandwidth limits that dataflow, so I'm dropping input-stationary.

Output-stationary had no stalls, so it stays the best candidate. Weight-stationary would need 180 passes over the array and would also move more data per pass, so it looks worse. Among the output-stationary shapes, 8×32 uses the array best: 36 output pixels fill 90% of 40 row slots, and 160 output channels fill exactly 5 groups of 32 columns. A 16×16 array fills only 75% of its rows (36 of 48 slots) but has a different per-pass overhead. Simulating 16×16 output-stationary will show whether the extra 3,200 or so cycles I saw on 8×32 depend on array shape.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 16×16 output-stationary design took 12,719 cycles with no stalls, so 8×32 output-stationary still leads at 11,409. Spread over passes, 8×32 costs about 456 cycles per pass (25 passes) and 16×16 about 424 (30 passes). Each pass costs roughly the 288-value filter window plus array fill and drain plus a fixed overhead of about 100 cycles. That makes the pass count the main factor. The other output-stationary shapes need more passes (32×8 needs 40, 8×16 needs 50, 8×8 needs 100), so none of them should beat 8×32.

The one dataflow I haven't simulated is weight-stationary. It needs 180 passes, but each pass streams only the 36 output pixels instead of 288 values. On a 16×16 array that is about 180 × (16 + 36 + 16) ≈ 12,200 cycles before overhead. That is probably worse than 11,409, but this dataflow's overhead is untested, and 16×16 is its cheapest shape, so I'll check it.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Going from height 16 to 8 cut 8 cycles. Both runs fit cycles ≈ H + W + K − 3 (16+64+64−3 = 141 and 8+64+64−3 = 133). Height 8 is the smallest allowed value. Width must stay at 64, because a narrower array would need two folds over N and roughly double the cycles. So 8×64 with output-stationary is the best OS option.

